<section class="business-section" id="phases">
  <div class="business-wrap">
    <div class="business-section-head">
      <div>
        <span class="business-eyebrow">The approach</span>
        <h2>The first ninety days, in order.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="business-section-intro">One phase depends on the last. I don't touch anything I haven't read, and I don't build an org before the delivery stops bleeding.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="business-phases">
      <div class="business-phase business-reveal" data-fill="0"><span class="business-when">Days 1 – 14</span><h3>Read the system</h3><p>Codebase, incident history, the last three sprints, and one-on-ones with everyone who ships. I change nothing and write down what I find, including where I'm wrong.</p></div>
      <div class="business-phase business-reveal" data-fill="1"><span class="business-when">Days 15 – 45</span><h3>Stop the bleeding</h3><p>Delivery cadence, review load, on-call, and the two or three architectural choices costing you a week every month. Small, reversible changes first.</p></div>
      <div class="business-phase business-reveal" data-fill="2"><span class="business-when">Days 46 – 90</span><h3>Build the org that ships</h3><p>A hiring bar, an ownership map, and a technical roadmap your CEO can defend to a board without me in the room.</p></div>
      <div class="business-phase business-reveal" data-fill="3"><span class="business-when">After</span><h3>Hand off, or stay light</h3><p>The goal is a team that doesn't need me. Most keep me one day a week for the decisions that are hard to reverse.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
